<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">3 February in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 3 February, from 1451 to 1959.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1451</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Murad II dies, and Mehmed II takes the Ottoman throne for good</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The death of Sultan Murad II brought his 18-year-old son Mehmed II back to power. Two years later Mehmed captured Constantinople, ending the Byzantine Empire.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1488</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bartolomeu Dias lands at Mossel Bay after rounding the Cape</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Having been driven round the southern tip of Africa by storms, Dias landed on the South African coast east of the Cape. His voyage showed that ships could sail from the Atlantic into the Indian Ocean.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1637</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Dutch tulip prices collapse</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At a bulb auction in Haarlem, buyers failed to appear and prices for tulip contracts crashed. 'Tulip mania' is often cited as the first recorded speculative bubble, though its damage is now thought modest.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1690</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Massachusetts Bay Colony issues paper money</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">To pay soldiers back from a failed expedition against Quebec, Massachusetts issued bills of credit, the first government paper money in the Americas (dated 1690 under the Old Style calendar).</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1830</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">London Protocol recognises an independent Greece</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Britain, France and Russia agreed that Greece should be a fully independent kingdom, after nearly a decade of revolution against the Ottomans. It was the first new state to emerge in the Balkans.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1870</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Fifteenth Amendment ratified, barring racial bars to voting</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The amendment forbade denying citizens the vote on grounds of race, colour or previous servitude. Southern states later evaded it with poll taxes, literacy tests and intimidation for almost a century.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1913</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sixteenth Amendment allows a US federal income tax</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Ratification of the Sixteenth Amendment gave Congress power to tax incomes without dividing the tax among states by population. Income tax became the main source of federal revenue.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1945</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Battle of Manila begins</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">American and Filipino forces entered Manila to retake it from the Japanese. The month-long battle destroyed much of the city, and around 100,000 Filipino civilians died, many in Japanese massacres.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1959</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Buddy Holly, Ritchie Valens and the Big Bopper die in a plane crash</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Their light aircraft crashed in snow near Clear Lake, Iowa, shortly after take-off, killing all aboard. Don McLean later called it 'the day the music died' in his song 'American Pie'.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a></div></li></ol>
